   461	def render(inst: dict) -> str:
   462	    p = inst["p"]
   463	    k = inst["k"]
   464	    factors = ", ".join(str(value) for value in inst["prime_divisors_of_k"])
   465	    matrix = inst["reference_matrix"]
   466	    m = inst["factor_count"]
   467	    statement = f"""Januarial trace-parameter certificate over a prime field
   468	
   469	Work in the field F_p of integers modulo the prime p={p}.  A 2-by-2 matrix is
   470	viewed projectively: two invertible matrices represent the same element of
   471	PGL(2,p) when one is a nonzero scalar multiple of the other.  Consequently a
   472	matrix has projective order k when its kth power is a nonzero scalar matrix and
   473	no smaller positive power is scalar.
   474	
   475	Here k=(p+1)/2={k}, whose distinct prime divisors are [{factors}].  The supplied
   476	reference matrix
   477	
   478	  Z = [[{matrix[0][0]}, {matrix[0][1]}],
   479	       [{matrix[1][0]}, {matrix[1][1]}]]  (entries modulo p)
   480	
   481	is promised to have projective order k.  For a nonzero residue theta in F_p,
   482	define
   483	
   484	  A_theta = [[0, -1], [theta, theta]] modulo p.
   485	
   486	Its determinant and trace are both theta, so its conjugacy invariant
   487	(trace(A_theta)^2 / determinant(A_theta)) modulo p is theta.  Call theta
   488	admissible exactly when A_theta has projective order k.  Equivalently, because
   489	the prime divisors of k are listed above, A_theta^k must be scalar and
   490	A_theta^(k/r) must be non-scalar for every listed prime r.
   491	
   492	Return one split monic polynomial H(T) as exactly {m} linear factors over F_p.
   493	Encode a factor T+c as the JSON pair [1,c], with c the canonical integer in
   494	0..p-1.  The decoded roots (-c modulo p) must be nonzero, pairwise distinct,
   495	and the factors must be ordered by increasing decoded root.  Every decoded
   496	root must be admissible.  Any {m} roots satisfying these rules are accepted;
   497	repeats are forbidden.
   498	
   499	Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags as a JSON list of exactly
   500	{m} pairs [[1,c1],[1,c2],...,[1,c{m}]].
   501	Example format: <answer>[[1,1],[1,2],[1,3]]</answer>
   502	Output nothing else inside the tags."""
   503	    mode = os.environ.get("GV_HINT_MODE")
   504	    if mode == "structural":
   505	        statement += "\n\nHint: " + STRUCTURAL_HINT
   506	    elif mode == "placebo":
   507	        statement += "\n\nHint: " + PLACEBO_HINT
   508	    return statement
