     1	# Januarial trace-parameter certificates (arXiv:1810.00203)
     2	
     3	Status: the generator and every local gate pass, but the required four-vendor
     4	hardening run is **not complete**. OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 “Key limit
     5	exceeded” for every attempted call on 2026-09-05. The error-only transcripts are
     6	preserved; they are not treated as evidence that a model failed the problem.
     7	
     8	| Profile field | Value |
     9	|---|---|
    10	| Track | B — an efficient exact algorithm exists |
    11	| Native domain | algebra |
    12	| Object regime | finite field |
    13	| Computational core | linear algebra |
    14	| Certificate form | polynomial (a canonical list of linear factors) |
    15	| Intended intuition | invariant: trace parameters of powers obey a second-order recurrence |
    16	| Domain essentiality | native |
    17	| Reduction | none |
    18	
    19	## What the family asks
    20	
    21	The source is Mehwish and Mushtaq, [“All januarials constructed from Hecke
    22	groups”](https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.00203). An instance gives a prime `p`,
    23	`k=(p+1)/2`, the prime divisors of `k`, and one native `2 x 2` matrix representing
    24	an order-`k` element of `PGL(2,p)`. The solver returns a split monic polynomial as
    25	12 factors `T+c`. Every decoded root `theta=-c` must make
    26	
    27	`A_theta = [[0,-1],[theta,theta]]`
    28	
    29	have exact projective order `k`. The verifier uses only modular matrix
    30	exponentiation: it checks that `A_theta^k` is scalar and that
    31	`A_theta^(k/r)` is non-scalar for every prime `r|k`. It accepts any 12 distinct
    32	valid roots and never reads the planted answer.
    33	
    34	This is the paper’s algebra, not a graph surrogate. Section 2, Theorems 1–2,
    35	identify `k=(p+1)/2` as the two-equal-orbit januarial regime. Section 3 defines
    36	`theta=(tr XY)^2/det(XY)` and its Cayley–Hamilton polynomial `g_k`; Theorem 3
    37	removes roots of the proper-divisor polynomials. The conjugacy lemma in Section 4
    38	says every order-`k` class occurs as `z^i` or `z^-i` for a coprime exponent, and
    39	the following count is `phi(k)/2`.
    40	
    41	## Why Track B
    42	
    43	There is no Track A claim: the paper itself supplies an efficient method. The
    44	strongest literal reference implementation scans `theta` values in order,
    45	performs the Section 3 exact-order test, and stops as soon as it has the required
    46	12. Its worst case is `O(p * omega(k) * log k)`. On the fixed hard audit
    47	instance (`seed=20260518`, `p=30637`) it found the twelfth root after 49 values,
    48	using 1,249 matrix multiplications and 19,984 counted modular scalar operations
    49	(0.0007–0.008 s across local runs). Across the eight G6 seeds its maximum was
    50	71,872 scalar operations. A separate full-field scan used 12,499,488 operations;
    51	that scan audits the exact density and is not presented as the task-solving
    52	baseline.
    53	
    54	The compact route is qualitatively different. If `w` is the eigenvalue ratio,
    55	then `theta=w+w^-1+2`; therefore `c_e=theta_e-2` satisfies
    56	`c_(e+1)=(theta-2)c_e-c_(e-1)`. Small exponents coprime to `k` produce the
    57	needed parameters. The audited route is bounded at 108 exact operations (190
    58	over all 256 hard seed slots). Seeing that invariant is the intended step; a
    59	literal field scan is far beyond unaided in-context execution.
    60	
    61	## Worked demo
    62	
    63	Here is the full rendered mathematical instance for
    64	`make_instance(n=31, seed=0, factor_count=3, seed_slots=1, seed_stride=2)`:
    65	
    66	```text
    67	Januarial trace-parameter certificate over a prime field
    68	
    69	Work in the field F_p of integers modulo the prime p=31. A 2-by-2 matrix is
    70	viewed projectively: two invertible matrices represent the same element of

